"Make a failure of the audit purge unable to stop the identity housekeeping job. Today supabase/migrations/20261044_audit_purge.sql defines audit_purge() (deletes audit_events older than 365 days, system caller only, uses the transaction-local setting app.audit_purge) and identity_housekeeping() calls it inside the same transaction as the sign-in attempt pruning, stale reservation release, orphan auth-user cleanup, invitation cleanup and import expiry. If the purge fails or times out (for example the first run against a large backlog of old rows) the whole housekeeping call rolls back and none of that cleanup runs. Fix it with a NEW forward-only migration (merged migrations are never edited): (1) in identity_housekeeping() run the purge inside a plpgsql BEGIN ... EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS block so a purge failure rolls back only the purge, the other cleanup still commits, and the result reports audit_events_purged 0 plus a flag such as audit_purge_failed true (no error text, no personal data); (2) cap how many rows one audit_purge() call deletes (for example the oldest 5000 by occurred_at, deleted by id) so a large backlog is worked off over several runs and one run never holds a long lock; keep audit_purge() signature, its system-caller-only right and the append-only trigger rule unchanged. Add database tests (tests/database, real local stack, run in CI): a failing purge does not stop the rest of the housekeeping (inject a failure the way other tests do) and is reported; the cap leaves the remaining aged rows for the next run; young rows and updates are still refused; the elevated-function catalogue test still passes. Update the U5 notes only where they say the purge is all-or-nothing. Source: findings R-18 of the U2 review and R-08 of the U5 review; plan group B1 in aidlc/spaces/default/intents/261012-real-backend-local/follow-ups/minor-findings-plan.md. Skip Reverse Engineering (the person already decided that for these fixes)."
